add(1223,'astar-priority','A-star queue priority','Node A has g=4,h=3, and node B has g=5,h=1. Which node does ordinary A-star prioritize when both are open and no other tie rule is relevant?',[
 ('B, because f(B)=6 is smaller than f(A)=7.','A-star compares total estimated route cost.'),('A, because g(A)=4 is smaller than g(B)=5.','That compares accumulated cost only, like a uniform-cost priority.'),('A, because h(A)=3 is larger.','A larger heuristic is not preferred.'),('Both must have the same priority because they share a goal.','Their accumulated and estimated remaining costs differ.')], 'The source’s lowest-cost wording should be read as lowest f=g+h, not lowest g alone.',typ='numerical',difficulty='medium',kind='code',formulas=[F_ASTAR],steps=['Compute f(A)=4+3=7.','Compute f(B)=5+1=6.'],refs=('code',))
add([1216,1223],'euclidean-lower-bound','When Euclidean distance is a valid heuristic','For unrounded Euclidean edge lengths on a geometric road graph, why is straight-line distance to the goal a lower bound on any path length?',[
 ('Repeated triangle inequalities bound a broken path below by the direct distance.','Detours cannot be shorter than the direct Euclidean segment under those costs.'),('It is valid only because every node has exactly four neighbors.','The property does not require grid degree four.'),('A lower bound must equal every complete path length.','Admissibility permits underestimation.'),('The largest single edge always equals the total remaining path.','A route can contain several edges of different lengths.')], 'The lower-bound proof depends on the cost metric; changing edge costs can invalidate it.',difficulty='medium',kind='code',formulas=[formula('||p_0−p_k|| <= sum_i ||p_i−p_(i+1)||',{'p_i':'path vertex position','k':'number of path edges'},{'p_i':'plot units','k':'count'},['Unrounded Euclidean metric; uniform positive scale.'])],refs=('code',))
add([1216,1223],'rounded-heuristic','Rounding can break admissibility','The code stores edge N2–N3 as2.00 but uses h(N2,N3)=sqrt(200²+10²)/100≈2.002498. What does this example show?',[
 ('The heuristic can slightly exceed the graph’s true remaining cost.','A direct path costs2.00, below the unrounded heuristic.'),('The heuristic is exactly consistent because the difference is small.','Consistency is an inequality, not a tolerance-free size judgment.'),('The stored graph has a negative edge weight.','The stored cost remains positive2.00.'),('A-star must return a wrong path for every start-goal pair.','Loss of a general guarantee does not prove failure on every case.')], 'The source mixes rounded graph costs with an unrounded heuristic; test results cannot restore a universal guarantee.',difficulty='medium',kind='code',formulas=[formula('Admissibility requires h(n)<=d*(n,goal)',{'h':'heuristic estimate','d*':'true shortest remaining graph cost','n':'node'},{'h':'demo km','d*':'demo km','n':'identifier'})],refs=('code',),conflict='Unrounded Euclidean h is not always a lower bound after edge costs are rounded down.')
add(1223,'empirical-vs-proof','Testing the source A-star','An author-added test compared the unchanged source A-star with Dijkstra on all144 ordered node pairs in its12-node graph and found equal path costs. What is justified?',[
 ('Those finite cases matched, despite a separate rounding-related heuristic guarantee gap.','Empirical agreement is bounded to the tested graph and cases.'),('The heuristic is thereby proven admissible on every geometric graph.','Finite observed route agreement does not prove that property.'),('All future traffic-cost changes preserve the same result without retesting.','New edge costs can invalidate the observation.'),('Dijkstra requires the same heuristic, so the comparison is necessarily circular.','Dijkstra can use zero heuristic and independently compute these nonnegative shortest costs.')], 'The recorded source-code experiment is useful evidence, not an unrestricted optimality proof.',difficulty='medium',kind='code',refs=('code',),assumptions=['This question states the actual recorded author test result, not a claim printed on the lecture page.'])
